{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/581579-circle-of-the-crone-in-new-york","id":581579,"title":"Circle of the Crone in New York","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Vampire: The Requiem"],"posts":[{"date":"2015-07-06T15:38:27-07:00","id":581579,"title":"Circle of the Crone in New York","content":"So I'm in a bit of a bind with the setting my group has been playing in. We've only had a few sessions so far and haven't gone into great detail about the Circle of the Crone in Brooklyn, where our game is taking place, but the problem is that I'm feeling <i>tremendously </i>uninspired in writing what the Circle are like in the borough and in the city at large. It's New York, so it's a very cosmopolitan setting that can pull on a lot of different traditions from around the world, so that's cool, but it can make it a little overwhelming to deal with.<br /> <br /> We have a very basic idea of how the Circle got its start in New York City: After they sort of had their breakout time in the 19th century. The Acolytes that came were part of the the wave that spread out through Western Europe and into the Americas after they rose up against the Sanctified in Scotland &amp; Ireland. They prompted a similar uprising among Kindred that were practicing disparate heretical traditions in New York. Previously, these individual cults or covens would get snapped up by the Sanctified who acted as watchdogs of a sort against nomads and newcomers to the Night City spreading their heresies, until they slaughtered a number of Sanctified and earned their place at the table through violence. They killed a lot of the Sanctified in New York until they were brought to heel, and for awhile, there was a truce negotiated between the Covenants.<br /> <br /> While it's still in effect today, the Sanctified have been weakened even further on a city-wide scale in the last 50 years, allowing the Circle to gain more prominence than its ever had in New York.<br /> <br /> So while we've got that cool backstory, we're sort of lacking in a compelling idea of what might make New York's Acolytes striking or interesting. This could be ideas for specific cults or it could be ideas for the overall organization of the Covenant in the city, I'm honestly shot for ideas.<br /> <br /> What would you do to make the Acolytes interesting?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I am no longer participating in the community. Please do not contact me about my previous work.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/159-leetsepeak","name":"Leetsepeak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4259"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-07-06T18:03:59-07:00","id":581729,"title":"","content":"I would have made them a religious cult that consisted of Quakers, Jews, Presbyterians Anabaptists etc. who were disillusioned with their faith by the Embrace but retained their contempt of Roman Catholicism and actively work to prevent its spread via the Sanctified. <br /> At the same time, I would have a faction with Santeria derived practices develop in the ranks of the Lancea Sanctum during the colonial period. They kept their sorcery and practices secret and avoided suspicion by actually hunting down and destroying other Cruac practitioners. They wouldn't have revealed their existence until the early 1980s, when they felt they were numerous enough to avoid being purged and would have also have long since insinuated themselves with a Carthian faction as more liberal than their pseudo Catholic counterparts.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5466-tsusasi","name":"tsusasi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5466_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1028"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-07-06T18:15:24-07:00","id":581738,"title":"","content":"Well, personally, I would throw out any relationship to a human religion. Instead, I focus around the concept of the Dark Mother, ala Beast, and the Strix. The default Circle of the Crone are basically the vampiric version of Wicca, with a touch of other neo-pagans. Go full out primordial monsters and dark gods with the Crone. It doesn't matter if we're talking Celtic, Norse, Voodoo, or whatever roots - each vampire in the Circle takes their own belief, and puts themselves as the god figure of a cult. <br /> <br /> In a lot of ways, the CotC shares a lot in common with the Brood - freedom and anarchy. Focus on that. There's no formal religion, only sharing of ideas to create a fusion of ideas that revere the Dark Mother. Everyone does their own thing, and they come together because they feel like a family united against all outsiders.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn","name":"MCN","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2102_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3479"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-07-06T18:52:00-07:00","id":581800,"title":"","content":"I used a dash of the Mother's Army from the Danse Macabre. Essentially in that, the Crone is a thing, theoretically a progenitor to the Kindred. And through the Cruac, witches can create her Vitae, which they then feed to the initiates during their entrance ceremony. From there, the Crone is sort of like the Beast, a voice in their soul, assuring them of certain deeds and balking at others. There's a certain amount of zealous aggression to this, too. They can hear Her voice, She is real. But the Lancea et Sanctum is the 'second state,' practically just due to being organized and structured longer. Other covenants get respect for outright denying the gift of the Crone or sidestepping it with politics and pseudoscience. It should seethe in some.<br /> <br /> The problem with the Mother's Army was that it was an actual army, so they weren't really in the game of 'playing' politics, their standard operating procedure is outright invasion. So, like I said, just a dash. Some of the members of the Circle are aggressive, they want to spread the word, and maybe in some settings they can. But others are more wise, they know that if other Kindred don't appreciate it first they won't understand, they'll take the gifts of Cruac without a deeper understanding of what they represent, and twist them to impure ends.<br /> <br /> In my own game the Circle was one of the weaker covenants in the beginning that became one of the most powerful, second to the Invictus, at the end. They started off with a schism, with the Mother of Scales ruling over it all as Hierarch, but not really leading the way she should. Essentially she was selfish, prideful, enjoyed her power and the freedom it gave her and didn't want to share it. So she kept telling the 'leader' of the younger ones, Krystal, that she wasn't ready for the next stage and so forth, as well as completely humiliating her on a political and social front in regards to the rest of the Court. Some other older members of the Circle thought she was being irresponsible about it, but as they were mostly independents and busy with their own things, they didn't have much clout in the politics of the covenant, and the Mother of Scales was probably one of the toughest vampires in the state so forcing the matter wouldn't get anywhere.<br /> <br /> Krystal persisted, however, the PCs helped a lot with her side of things. And eventually their actions got the Mother of Scales killed, allowing Krystal to rise up and put things in order so the knowledge of Cruac could be dispensed among the coven as it was meant to be. <br /> <br /> Things like that help. Not always thinking about covenants as one big monolith but just a bunch of people loosely tied to one another. Best way to make the Circle interesting is to make the members interesting. Then figure out some interesting personalities, squabbles, 'civil' wars, see how those things would turn the Acolytes into the ones in your game.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-07-07T10:46:28-07:00","id":582495,"title":"","content":"Well, if the original Acolytes came from Ireland and Scotland then the cult would likely have a Celtic flavor; worship of Morrigan, a high priestess called the Caillagh ny Gueshag, etc. Of course this would put it in contrast to the complex modern demographics of Brooklyn. Maybe that contrast could be the hook to make them interesting; Brooklyn being the only place in world you can find Kindred Ancillae of Caribbean and Puerto Rican descent worshipping Celtic goddesses.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6617-vyrkolash","name":"Vyrkolash","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"60"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-07-07T14:00:47-07:00","id":582703,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Leetsepeak</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/581579-circle-of-the-crone-in-new-york#post581579\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So while we've got that cool backstory, we're sort of lacking in a compelling idea of what might make New York's Acolytes striking or interesting. This could be ideas for specific cults or it could be ideas for the overall organization of the Covenant in the city, I'm honestly shot for ideas.</div> </div> </div> </div>You might want to have a look at &quot;American Gods&quot; by Neil Gaiman. He plays with the idea how the belief in different gods from all parts of the world changed when the respective believers came to America. While he looks at it from the perspective of the gods themselves, not the worshippers, you might still find some inspiration there.<br /> I remember one or two scenes specifically in New York. I think it was something with a Djinn driving a taxi.<br /> An apart from that, it's one of the best novels I've ever read.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6817-quinn","name":"Quinn","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"29"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-07-07T15:30:54-07:00","id":582809,"title":"","content":"Thanks for all the ideas so far folks.<br /> <br /> I've actually started reading American Gods and really enjoyed it so far, that's actually an unconventional suggestion that I really dig.<br /> <br /> The stuff that we've initially developed has used human gods and goddesses, but typically the Kindred are invoking a twisted and sort of ahistorical aspect of the deities. Like one of the rough sketched out ideas was a tripartite goddess thing like the Maiden, the Mother and the Crone (or the Fates, the Norns, etc.) called the Three Sisters as one of the primary cults. I was looking at practices from a couple of different pagan religions to assimilate into the cult, with the Three Sisters being the primary power in New York's circle and ruled over by Kindred who have embodied their deities (sometimes through literal transformation, as is the case with the Kindred who embodies the Crone, she uses Cruac to maintain the appearance of an elderly figure.) Do you guys think any of that sounds cool, or is it a bit eh?<br /> <br /> I also had an idea where the way their &quot;religion&quot; might work is based on individual members having dreams during the daysleep, or possibly mortal followers who get dreams that convert them into followers of the Three Sisters, but it felt a bit out there, and I wasn't sure how the Circle relates to mortals or if they have mortal cultists.<br /> <br /> I bring this up because I like MCN's idea, if nothing else than because I'd prefer to not get &quot;real&quot; cult religion wrong, if something like that is really possible, but I <i>do </i>like the concepts of some of these deities reimagined by Kindred for their belief structure, as I think having a plurality of divinities really lends itself to the weird and fractured nature of the Circle. I guess I'm concerned about reducing it to the Dark Mother or the Strix because these already feel like deliberately syncretic figures who I'd much rather evoke through a particular aspect than just having them generally worshipping them.<br /> <br /> It's kind of like how a Roman might think of the Strix as the Striges (duh!) or how people in Tokyo might try to identify them with Tengu. Or how the Dark Mother might be aspected as a Tiamat like serpent goddess in one cult, or like a Lilith-like rebel lady in another. I think that's the appeal to me for these sorts of things, does that make any sense?<br /> <br /> Nofather, I think that idea is an interesting one, but I'm not too keen on the Crone as a distinct force. It's sort of like how I think the <i>least </i>interesting question behind the Lancea et Sanctum is &quot;does God actually support what they're doing?&quot; because their beliefs aren't interesting because of on-high metaphysical whatever, it's cool because of what it says about them, what they'd do on behalf of those beliefs, how others receive them, etc.<br /> <br /> I don't think it really matters if the Circle is right, so much as what their myriad beliefs mean and how they construct an organization that bridges so many gaps with an ideology, even if practices and some beliefs differ.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I am no longer participating in the community. Please do not contact me about my previous work.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/159-leetsepeak","name":"Leetsepeak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4259"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-07-08T07:53:01-07:00","id":583469,"title":"","content":"\u200bThree lines of thought (I'm sorry.The second one got a bit wordy):<br /> <br /> <b>1. Trinity Goddess</b><br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Leetsepeak</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/581579-circle-of-the-crone-in-new-york?p=582809#post582809\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I was looking at practices from a couple of different pagan religions to assimilate into the cult, with the Three Sisters being the primary power in New York's circle and ruled over by Kindred who have embodied their deities (sometimes through literal transformation, as is the case with the Kindred who embodies the Crone, she uses Cruac to maintain the appearance of an elderly figure.) Do you guys think any of that sounds cool, or is it a bit eh?</div> </div> </div> </div>I like it. This Maiden, Mother, Crone trinity really has something to it. I used a similar idea to replace Octavio, the prophet of Transylvania Chronicles, in my game by three female Kolduns inspired by a slavic Trinity of mythical figures: The Zorya. I blogged about it: <a href=\"https://vampirestoryteller.wordpress.com/2014/04/30/storytelling-tc-change-the-prophet/\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>. Probably too tailored for Transylvania to be of use for you, but maybe you can cherrypick some elements that you like.<br /> <br /> <b>2. Assimilation Strategies</b><br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Leetsepeak</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/581579-circle-of-the-crone-in-new-york?p=582809#post582809\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I don't think it really matters if the Circle is right, so much as what their myriad beliefs mean and how they construct an organization that bridges so many gaps with an ideology, even if practices and some beliefs differ.</div> </div> </div> </div>\u200bWhen I first read this, it reminded me of something I couldn't quite pin down. So it's been on my mind for half a day, and now I remembered.<br /> <br /> Years ago, I wrote an essay about empires and mythology. I'll spare you the details, but the basic idea was that in the Bronze Age, military technology made a leap forward. Not only in regard to metallurgy but in regard to logistics. Some cultures figured out how to organize a supply train for large armies. Up to that point, warfare is only possible at the fringes of your territory. The people there have had cultural exchange with your people for a long time and probably have a culture and mythology similar to yours.<br /> <br /> With that supply train technology (technology in the original sense of &quot;know how&quot;), you can make war on people who live farther away and who's culture differs significantly from yours. You can conquer large territories and thus forge the first empires (think Babylonians, Assyrians, Egyptians etc.).<br /> <br /> These first empires were tasked with a new challenge: They had to assimilate a culture that differed significantly from theirs. Most importantly, they had to make the &quot;new people&quot; accept the legitimacy of their leadership. Since leadership was almost exclusively charismatic (&quot;by the Grace of the Gods&quot;, so to speak), this meant you had to assimilate their mythology.<br /> <br /> So, long story short, I came up with four possible strategies to deal with this task:<ul><li>Identification<br /> You make a claim that elements of the the mythologies are identical. It essentially means to say: &quot;Yes, your sun god and our sun god is the same guy, we just call him differently.&quot;<br /> The Romans did that a lot. They had veritable translation tables. The Phrygian goddess Cybele and the Egyptian Hathor are also good examples for this.</li> <li>Diversification<br /> You try to incorporate the foreign mythology in your own system. It essentially means to say: &quot;Yes, your sun god is real, too. We acknowledge that. He is the friend/assistant/son of our sun god.&quot; You find that a lot in the Greek pantheon. The old sun god Hyperion got integrated as a servant of the new Helios.<br /> Sometimes the narrative was more martial: &quot;We conquered you and our gods conquered your gods.&quot; That variant can be found in Norse mythology, too. The Vanir are leftovers of a pantheon that got assimilated into the \u00c6sir.</li> <li>Unification/Reinvention<br /> You concentrate on the common mythemes that the two mythologies you want to assimilate have and merge them or create a new one that embodies that common core. Egyptian Isis Hathor and Roman Sol Invictus are good examples for that.</li> <li>Abstraction<br /> This came much later and is the main idea behind Gnosticism. It essentially means to say: &quot;We mortals can not have true knowledge (gnosis) about the gods. They can only show us masks and representations of themselves. The real divine sun has revealed itself to us in the guise of our sun god and to you in the mask of your sun god.&quot;</li> </ul><br /> <br /> <i>Now, finally to the Circle in New York :-)</i><br /> <br /> With the constant influx of immigrants from all over the world, the Circle faced a similar task as the ancient empires: assimilate the mythologies they brought with them. Over time, probably all the strategies above have been tried.<br /> <br /> So, if you want to come up with something that no longer corresponds to a mortal religion, you might use these strategies to build some syncretic amalgam.<br /> <br /> If I had to come up with something, I'd assume two main lines of assimilations within the Circle, which now begin to merge:<ul><li>The first and older line corresponds to European immigrants. Its cultic practice is an amalgam of Celtic, Norse and Greco-Roman mythologies. All centered around Gaia, Mother Earth.</li> <li>The second line corresponds to the Asian immigrants. Its practice is some mixture of Asian traditions, dominated by Chinese folk religion. Lots of Dragons, here.</li> </ul><br /> Building on the Chinese <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dilong\" target=\"_blank\">Earth Dragon</a>, the Greek <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_%28mythology%29\" target=\"_blank\">Python</a>, the Welsh (and Arthurian) <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Welsh_Dragon\" target=\"_blank\">Red Dragon</a> a merging process has started some decades ago. Especially Python is interesting here, because in Greek Mythology she was bound to Gaia and was killed by Apollo who is a god of light (and in <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Helios#Helios_and_Apollo\" target=\"_blank\">later Greek Mythology a sun god</a>).<br /> <br /> The result is a syncretic cult of the &quot;Mother Dragon&quot; who was an enemy of the sun god.<br /> <br /> Something like that?<br /> <br /> <b>3. Acolyte Boheme</b><br /> My favourite idea, however, is that the &quot;Semioticians&quot; found in the Covenant book are particularly numerous in New York's Circle of the Crone. They are drawn to the &quot;abstraction&quot; strategy mentioned above. For a New York flavour of them, I like the idea of intellectual hipster vampires hanging out in Greenwich Village coffee shops, discussing mythology, quoting Joseph Campbell and Georges Dum\u00e9zil and looking everything but sinister with their iPads. But when push comes to shove, they pull a vast diversity of Babylonian, Celtic, Chinese, Indian and Cabbalistic rituals out of their pockets and start wreaking havoc with their blood sorcery ;-)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6817-quinn","name":"Quinn","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"29"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-07-08T09:48:22-07:00","id":583550,"title":"","content":"The threat of the Sanctified provided most of the unifying force for the Crone and with them growing weaker the Circle may begin to polarize, splitting into factions based around culture and background, or around less esoteric differences like one group wanting to finish of the Sanctified, another that argues they should hold off longer until they are strong enough to take the entire city, and the last who want to truly be accepted by the other clans.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6084-2pttakrill","name":"2ptTakrill","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"942"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-07-08T10:14:35-07:00","id":583562,"title":"","content":"Wow, that's an awesome post. I'm definitely gonna refer back to these if I'm ever making a Circle again, and hell probably for the Cult of Augurs too.<br /> <br /> I've been working with the idea that the reason the Three Sisters provide such leadership to the cult is because they're readers of prophecy <i>par excellence </i>and that the Circle of New York puts great heed in their prophecies, so much so that when they do their big auguries, they're sort of Circle celebrations.<br /> <br /> I like the idea of the Mother Dragon, though. I think I'm sticking with the Three Sisters, if nothing else than in an advisory role to the other cults. They make prophecy, and others choose to heed it or ignore it at their own peril.<br /> <br /> What kind of name would you give to the Mother Dragon's cult?<br /> <br /> I love the Semioticians, and I'm definitely gonna implement them as a sub-culture among all the Acolytes in New York.<br /> <br /> The other thing I'd love some thoughts about is where and how the Circle might integrate mortal cultists, if they do so at all.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I am no longer participating in the community. Please do not contact me about my previous work.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/159-leetsepeak","name":"Leetsepeak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4259"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-07-08T12:12:39-07:00","id":583726,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Leetsepeak</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/581579-circle-of-the-crone-in-new-york?p=583562#post583562\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I've been working with the idea that the reason the Three Sisters provide such leadership to the cult is because they're readers of prophecy <i>par excellence </i>and that the Circle of New York puts great heed in their prophecies, so much so that when they do their big auguries, they're sort of Circle celebrations.<br /> <br /> I like the idea of the Mother Dragon, though. I think I'm sticking with the Three Sisters, if nothing else than in an advisory role to the other cults. They make prophecy, and others choose to heed it or ignore it at their own peril.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, if you want to go for prophecy, you might actually use both ideas.<br /> The Python might be a keystone for this. So, a bit of background info: In Greek mythology that dragon was said to have the power of prophecy. And fittingly enough, the prophetic power was in its <i>blood</i>. In fact, when Apollo slew this &quot;earth dragon of Delphi&quot; near its lair at Mount Parnassus, the soil there was soaked in its blood. From then on, the earth gave off noxious fumes that brought prophetic visions. That's why the Oracle of Delphi was located there. The chief priestess of the oracle was called the &quot;Pythia&quot; because she wielded the prophetic power that came from the Python.<br /> <br /> You could use both the Triple Goddess and the Earth dragon by doing it like George R.R. Martin and simply say &quot;The dragon has three heads&quot;.<br /> <br /> Let's say this cult is built around this Earth/Mother Dragon and the Maiden, the Mother and the Crone are aspects of the dragon. The cult could be lead by three Kindred that embody these three aspects.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Leetsepeak</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/581579-circle-of-the-crone-in-new-york?p=583562#post583562\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">What kind of name would you give to the Mother Dragon's cult?</div> </div> </div> </div>What about &quot;The Three-headed Python&quot;, or the &quot;Pythiae&quot;?<br /> I'm imagining them having a powerful Cr\u00faac ritual where all three of them spill their blood to the ground. The vitae starts to boil as it comes into contact with the earth (it draws the heat of the dragon's fire in the earth's core) and turns into fumes that bring prophetic visions. In game mechanics, this could work similar to the Auspex powers, granting a number of questions to ask the storyteller.<br /> <br /> Come to think of it, maybe they have a sacred place of power in NY, which coincidentally is also one of the &quot;Dragon's Nests&quot; the Ordo is always seeking. Might make a nice hook for conflict or cooperatione between the two covenants.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Leetsepeak</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/581579-circle-of-the-crone-in-new-york?p=583562#post583562\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I love the Semioticians, and I'm definitely gonna implement them as a sub-culture among all the Acolytes in New York.</div> </div> </div> </div>Cool. I'm glad you like it.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Leetsepeak</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/581579-circle-of-the-crone-in-new-york?p=583562#post583562\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The other thing I'd love some thoughts about is where and how the Circle might integrate mortal cultists, if they do so at all.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, if they do, they might always have an eye on the esoteric/neopagan scene in NY; or even encourage for groups like that to form in the respective subcultures. Once in a while, they might introduce a select few to the deeper mysteries. But frankly, I'd leave the mortals out of it and only use them for the occasional sacrifice. You know how it is with these old, dark deities (and especially with dragons): they need to be fed, once in a while ;-)<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6817-quinn","name":"Quinn","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"29"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-07-10T13:55:41-07:00","id":586424,"title":"","content":"In my VTR version of NYC (which I've posted about previously), I liked the idea of the Crones operating as several different wildly divergent cults each operating under their own set of historical principles and all maintaining something of a mutual defense pact. Given that the cults are spread rather thin across each of the Boroughs, and each Borough is largely under its own separate form of leadership, this allowed different groups to diverge greatly over the centuries.<br /> <br /> I figured major idealogies would be:<br /> <br /> -Thelema/Theosophy/Neo-Egyptologist<br /> -Voodoo/Santeria<br /> -Traditional Native American Beliefs<br /> -Celtic/Wiccan<br /> <br /> I must say though, I do like the idea of their being a unifying figure/figures which each of the groups refer to under different names, but ultimately holds quite a bit of moral (if not actual) authority over the City's Crones.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5106-umoja","name":"Umoja","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"31"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-07-15T08:48:46-07:00","id":591377,"title":"","content":"So I was reading a sci fi RPG game from Gurps called Infinite Worlds or more specifically its predecessor Alternate Earths. One of those worlds features an early discovery of America. One of the cuter ideas is the free port of &quot;Manannan&quot; on the sight of New York, an island kingdom settled by Irish who conflated the name of their sea god Manannan with the native name Manhattan. <br /> <br /> All I'm saying is there was a major influx of Irish immigrants into the city in the 1840s. If your looking for another layer to the Circle, you could have some of their vampires come to the same conclusion about the island's name. Perhaps even believing the god sleeps in some hidden cave well below the city streets?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2244-nothri","name":"nothri","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"485"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-07-15T23:58:26-07:00","id":592379,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nothri</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/581579-circle-of-the-crone-in-new-york?p=591377#post591377\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So I was reading a sci fi RPG game from Gurps called Infinite Worlds or more specifically its predecessor Alternate Earths. One of those worlds features an early discovery of America. One of the cuter ideas is the free port of &quot;Manannan&quot; on the sight of New York, an island kingdom settled by Irish who conflated the name of their sea god Manannan with the native name Manhattan.<br /> <br /> All I'm saying is there was a major influx of Irish immigrants into the city in the 1840s. If your looking for another layer to the Circle, you could have some of their vampires come to the same conclusion about the island's name. Perhaps even believing the god sleeps in some hidden cave well below the city streets?</div> </div> </div> </div>That reminds me of something I saw MANY years ago, about the Mankato tribe selling the island so such a low price, because they knew what was sleeping under it...","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://www.nodiatis.com/personality.htm\" target=\"_blank\"><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://www.nodiatis.com/pub/12.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /></a></div> &quot;A free society is one in which it is safe to be unpopular.&quot;<br /> <span style=\"color:DarkRed\"><span style=\"font-size:20px\"><span style=\"font-family:Comic Sans MS\">I find <a href=\"https://www.google.com/url?sa=t&amp;rct=j&amp;q=&amp;esrc=s&amp;source=web&amp;cd=3&amp;cad=rja&amp;uact=8&amp;ved=0ahUKEwin4NXQzLfXAhVGySYKHV3TDXYQFgg3MAI&amp;url=http%3A%2F%2Fwww.ign.com%2Farticles%2F2017%2F04%2F25%2Fmarvels-nick-spencer-on-captain-americas-betrayal-and-handling-fan-reactions&amp;usg=AOvVaw2EcsX7n5ebntygb0TizyGl\" target=\"_blank\">Nick Spencer</a> and <a href=\"https://www.google.com/url?sa=t&amp;rct=j&amp;q=&amp;esrc=s&amp;source=web&amp;cd=4&amp;cad=rja&amp;uact=8&amp;ved=0ahUKEwiK7d7szLfXAhXCWSYKHTpKBbcQFgg4MAM&amp;url=https%3A%2F%2Fen.wikipedia.org%2Fwiki%2FRichard_B._Spencer&amp;usg=AOvVaw1tuP6phfINia-bh_jWsDT_\" target=\"_blank\">Richard Spencer</a> to be equally repulsive.</span></span></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6138-mangle77","name":"Mangle77","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6138_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"605"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}